WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.217
Single ownership is Rust's default,

2
00:00:02.297 --> 00:00:04.677
but real systems sometimes need shared
ownership.

3
00:00:04.757 --> 00:00:06.419
`Box` provides owned indirection.

4
00:00:06.499 --> 00:00:09.212
`Rc` supports shared ownership in
single-threaded code.

5
00:00:09.292 --> 00:00:11.617
`Arc` supports shared ownership across
threads.

6
00:00:11.697 --> 00:00:14.798
These tools solve real design problems
when chosen deliberately.

7
00:00:14.878 --> 00:00:16.871
Shared ownership changes cleanup timing.

8
00:00:16.951 --> 00:00:20.717
A value behind `Rc` or `Arc` is dropped
only when the final strong owner goes away.

9
00:00:20.797 --> 00:00:24.728
That prevents ordinary use-after-free in
safe Rust because the value remains alive

10
00:00:24.808 --> 00:00:26.082
while strong owners exist,

11
00:00:26.162 --> 00:00:29.705
but it also means lifecycle and cleanup
timing may be less obvious than with a

12
00:00:29.785 --> 00:00:30.450
single owner.

13
00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:33.741
`Weak` references model non-owning
relationships that may expire.

14
00:00:33.821 --> 00:00:36.424
A weak reference does not keep the value
alive by itself.

15
00:00:36.504 --> 00:00:39.715
Code must check whether the value is still
available before using it.

16
00:00:39.795 --> 00:00:43.616
This is useful for parent-child
relationships, caches, observer lists, graphs,

17
00:00:43.696 --> 00:00:47.849
and lifecycle-sensitive designs where not
every relationship should extend lifetime.

18
00:00:47.929 --> 00:00:51.528
Reference cycles are usually leaks rather
than use-after-free in safe Rust,

19
00:00:51.608 --> 00:00:53.435
but they are still lifecycle problems.

20
00:00:53.515 --> 00:00:57.170
If strong references keep each other alive
forever, cleanup may never happen.

21
00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:00.572
That can matter for memory, file handles,
network connections, locks,

22
00:01:00.652 --> 00:01:01.981
and long-running services.

23
00:01:02.061 --> 00:01:05.494
Interior mutability and shared ownership
should be reviewed carefully.

24
00:01:05.574 --> 00:01:06.737
They can be appropriate,

25
00:01:06.817 --> 00:01:10.083
but they move some reasoning into runtime
checks or synchronization.

26
00:01:10.163 --> 00:01:13.153
The design should explain who owns the
value, who can observe it,

27
00:01:13.233 --> 00:01:15.061
who can mutate it, how cleanup happens,

28
00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:18.020
and what happens when a weak relationship
no longer upgrades.
